WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:23.220
Life on Earth emerged at least three and a
half billion years ago as prokaryotes.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.900
These are the simple unicellular organisms.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:33.860
They have a membrane on the outside and a
wash of cellular machinery inside, all mixed

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.300
together and touching, and sharing the same
environment.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.080
This worked, certainly.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.780
Life chugged along this way for nearly half
of the history of life on earth.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:52.560
But then, 1.8 billion years ago, something
remarkable happened.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.600
Something that led to a tremendous shift in
the scope and complexity of life.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:06.440
Something we should all be grateful for because,
without that leap, we would not exist.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.120
Cells started to...contain cells.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.500
Now this isn’t generally how it’s talked
about in science class.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.310
There you hear that Eukaryotes have quote
“membrane-bound organelles.”

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:26.840
These are areas of the cell that are separated
from the rest of the cytoplasm by membranes,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:33.200
just as the cell itself is separated from
the rest of the universe by its membrane.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:39.170
It turns out, different activities require
different conditions, and these cells within

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.840
cells allow for those different conditions.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:51.120
That, in short, is the secret of Eukaryotic
success.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:54.090
But how did it happen?

00:01:54.090 --> 00:02:01.939
Well, over decades of study we have determined
something shockingly peculiar.

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:07.909
Something so odd that it makes us kind of
mad that we now discuss it as if it isn’t

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:09.970
the miracle it is.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:20.210
1.8 billion years ago, a cell consumed another
cell...but then it didn’t digest it...it

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:28.640
let it reproduce inside of it, and they lived
together, and, over time, became the same

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.280
organism.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.040
Or did they?

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:50.380
This is what we call “Endosymbiotic Theory.”

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:56.040
Mitochondrion appeared when the consumed cell
was adapted to live in an oxygen rich environment

00:02:56.049 --> 00:03:00.680
and chloroplasts appeared when the swallowed
cell was photosynthetic.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:06.310
This idea was deeply controversial when it
was first proposed, but as data have continued

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:12.569
to come in, endosymbiotic theory has been
able to explain more and more about the realities

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:13.569
we find.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:19.549
For example, that chloroplasts have their
own DNA which they use to create the proteins

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:21.370
required for their function.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:28.360
And as we dive deeper into the microcosmos,
it just becomes obvious that this happens.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:37.700
This is Paramecium bursaria, a single-celled
protozoa, that has several hundred algal cells

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:43.890
from the genus Chlorella living in its own cytoplasm, making it green.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:49.959
The algae live inside Paramecium bursaria
providing it with fuel in the form of sugar

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:53.350
and other substances produced via photosynthesis.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:59.400
And Paramecium bursaria provides protection
for the algae from algae eaters and viruses.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:07.680
P. bursaria is regarded as a predatory protozoa,
it feeds on bacteria, small organisms, and, yes, algae

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:13.299
and because of that it's often
thought that the algae in it are temporary symbionts

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:16.690
engulfed by Paramecium bursaria’s feeding
behavior.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:23.260
But in fact, many other protozoa acquire algae
in that manner for temporary use, but that

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:30.830
is not the case for P. bursaria; its symbionts
are continuously inherited from generation

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.170
to generation through cell division.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:39.010
The symbiotic Chlorella guide the Paramecium
to well lit areas, so they can photosynthesize

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.010
more efficiently.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:44.940
The mutual relationship is extremely beneficial
for the Paramecium.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:51.030
Even when the Chlorella-containing Paramecium cells are put in nutrition-free saline solution they

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:59.460
can survive for more than 3 months while cells that didn't have Chlorella died within a week!

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:07.680
This is another single-celled organism with
endosymbiotic algae, it's a testate amoeba.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.530
A kind of amoeba that builds itself a shell.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:16.510
This species, like some kind of sculptural
artist, pulls bits and pieces of mineral from

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.940
its environment to create these amazing looking
homes.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:26.750
You can see the amoeba extending from the
opening of the shell and you can see the green

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.540
algae in its cytoplasm.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:35.440
Just like Paramecium bursaria, the algae
use sunlight to produce food sharing it with the

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.170
amoeba while the amoeba provides protection.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:46.350
Some unicellular organisms don't need oxygen
for growth, indeed the presence of free oxygen

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.620
can affect them negatively or even kill them.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.250
These organisms are known as anaerobes.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.620
Such as this one, Metopus.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:06:01.190
It is an anaerobic ciliate we find in pond
sediment and it has an endosymbiotic relationship

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.220
with methanogenic archaea.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:10.370
Now we haven’t talked much on this channel about archeans, but they are the third domain of life, along

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:16.580
with bacteria and eukaryotes and, like bacteria, they are prokaryotic.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.030
We can’t wait to do our episode on them someday soon.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:26.000
Many of the single-celled eukaryotes living
in anaerobic environments contain symbiotic

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.580
prokaryotes, some of these prokaryotes are
methanogens, meaning they can use free Hydrogen

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:35.820
to generate energy and methane.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.360
The advantages of having these symbionts are not fully understood

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.410
but while Metopus can live without the symbionts

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:47.300
they grow faster when they have them.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:51.420
Endosymbiosis occurs in multicellular organisms
as well.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:56.080
This is a freshwater relative of jellyfish
and sea anemones, Hydra!

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.400
It's simply stuffed full of algal endosymbionts.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.490
We collected this Hydra from a nearby pond
and cultured it in our aquarium.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:10.950
The benefits provided by the symbiotic relationship here have been well documented, with scientists

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:17.500
actually tracking how carbon moves from the
environment, into the algea, and then into

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:23.010
the hydra, and studies have shown that up
to 69% of the caloric requirements of the

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:27.580
hydra is satisfied by its algal symbionts.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.400
Nice.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:43.000
So, we see, some organisms temporarily pull
in symbionts, others pass them from generation

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.440
to generation.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:47.300
Some can survive without them, and some cannot.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:53.380
When we look at the algal cells in P Bursaria,
we’re forced to ask if those cells

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:59.060
are part of the organism, or if they’re
simply cells of one species living in the

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:00.060
cells of another.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:06.830
If that’s the case, it’s worth asking
whether the mitochondria in you are you at

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:15.140
all, or if they are just another extremely successful species of prokaryote that is particularly reliant on

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.300
its host cell.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:24.500
As we look deeper and deeper down, the line
between organisms is harder and harder to find.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:30.720
Which is why, if you think hard enough you might begin to feel like our cells are more

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.680
than just ourselves.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:41.010
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds...and

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.700
inhabits us.

